  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1147 AM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nClear, sunny skies greet us this morning as we head into what should \nbe our warmest day this week. The upper level ridge that's been \noverhead the last couple days continues to sit over the Rockies, \nkeeping subsidence and downsloping winds over the area. Thanks to \nwarmer temperatures in the mid-levels (850-700mb) and those \ndownsloping winds, temperatures today should reach into the upper \n80s for much of the urban corridor and plains, with high temps \npossibly reaching into the low 90s for portions of the northern \nplains. The record high temperature for Denver on October 5 is 87F, \nand it's likely (~70%) that we'll at least tie that record today.\n\nTomorrow's temps will continue to trend above normal, though a few \ndegrees cooler than today thanks to some expected cloud cover. A \nweak disturbance in the ridge Tuesday and Wednesday could bring the \nchance for isolated showers over the higher elevations in the \nafternoon both days, mainly in Park and Clear Creek Counties. The \nrest of the week should remain warm and dry, with temperatures \nstaying about 10-15 degrees above normal through Saturday. \n\nAs of the latest model guidance, Sunday should be when we start to \ncool back off, with a cooler, possibly wetter pattern settling in by \nMonday as an upper-level trough pushes into the region. While models \nare in fairly good agreement on the timing of this trough, there's a \nlot of uncertainty as to how deep the trough becomes and how much \nmoisture it pulls in, both of which will affect what weather impacts \nwe see next Monday and Tuesday. Interestingly, the latest ECMWF runs \nhave the remnants of what is currently Hurricane Rachel getting \ndragged to the northeast and pulled into the area along the \ndownstream side of the trough. The GFS, on the other hand, keeps \nHurricane Rachel on a northwesterly path, away from any interaction \nwith the trough, which could mean we see less significant weather \nimpacts associated with this system.",
